A||||5||Hock||Bigger Fuel Pump
Z||000000||Hock||06-03-99||11:25 AM||||I have a question about my fuel pump. First let me explain what I am doing. I am building up an Iron Duke for my 85 Fiero. This engine already has a Comp Cam 260H camshaft and will hopefully be receiving a Holley 390 four barrel carb and a Clifford intake. My question is: will the factory TBI fuel pump have enough fuel flow for this engine? I am expecting to have somewhere between 175-200hp when the engine is done. Besides what I already mentioned there will be other things done to the engine to help reach this goal. Also, what gears would be better to run, 3.35 or 3.60's? I want my top end but want it to be quicker out of the turns. So what ever help anybody can give me would be apreciated.
Z||000001||Stroker||06-07-99||09:47 PM||||Hock, the stock fuel pump should be perfectly adequate for your buildup, cosidering that my buddy Ed Parks at The Fiero Factory uses the 4-cylinder Fiero's fuel pump (which, by the way, is rated at 9-13 psi) on V-8, yes, V-8 conversions. The theory(which works quite well) is that it's easier to take 9-13 psi and use a pressure regulator to break it down to 5.5-6 psi at a carburetor (on the V-8) than to take 38-41 psi on the V-6 and break it down to the same pressure. Cosidering that a TBI is nothing more than a glorified carburetor, your stocker should work fine. You could even use an aftermarket fuel pressure regulator with a gauge at the TBI so you can get a visual of what's goin' on. Let us all know!
Z||000002||Stroker||06-07-99||09:52 PM||||Hock! I almost forgot! Use the 3.60 (3.65) gears. They are better off the turns and still give you good mileage. Truthfully, if I were you I'd opt for the 4.10 4-speed from an '84 4- cyl. They're Greeeeeaat!!!
Z||000003||Carrolles||06-08-99||06:44 AM||||Ed Parks has the 410 gears in his 85SE 2m6.  I've driven the car and it is great.  Also he actually gets better highway mileage that I do in my 85SE 2m6 with the 4spd with 365 gears.  At 70mph my car is turning around 3000 rpm.  His is turning around 3400 rpm which puts it at the top of the torque curve where the engine likes to run.
Z||000004||Stroker||06-08-99||02:11 PM||||Hock, Last night I forgot to address your concern about top speed using the lower geared transaxle. I'll work the numbers and give you exact(or nearly exact) speeds with both transaxles so you can make the determination. The difference is not going to be that great. S
Z||000005||Stroker||06-11-99||07:30 PM||||This is for HOCK, and ANYBODY ELSE who just HAS to know what the top speed POTENTIAL is for the gears he was asking about.<br>First of all, these are POTENTIAL SPEEDS!<br>They are only attainable with LOTS of power (and a race track, of course).<p>A 3.35 in 5th gear w/24.2" tire diameter at a 6000 rpm redline will run to 174MPH / 188MPH 6500 rpm redline.<p>A 3.35 w/25.7" T.D at 6000 rpm redline will run to 185MPH / 200MPH at 6500 rpm redline.<p>A 3.61 in 5th gear w/24.2" T.D at 6000 rpm will run to 166MPH / 180MPH at 6500 rpm redline.<p>A 3.61 w/25.7" T.D. at 6000 rpm redline will run to 176MPH / 191MPH at 6500 rpm redline.<p>These are all PRETTY FAST!!!!!<p>Hock, if you ever really go that fast  let us know how! <br>If your gonna run one of THESE 5 speeds, then DEFINITELY go with the 3.61!!!<br>It has the best of both worlds!!<p>
